{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTKYCoZhQcvizpgt6WsE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015MY9446EhADdJVZ1rjZUmh","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/gradient_masking_safety_20261027_120000_ab12cd34/README.md","content":"# Do safety-tuned LLMs refuse on keywords or on meaning?\n\nWe test whether safety tuning in open chat models (0.5B–32B; Qwen2.5, Qwen3, Llama-3.x, Llama-2, Phi-3, Mistral,\nGemma-2) works like \"gradient masking\": a defence keyed to surface patterns that simple distribution shifts get around.\nEvery model gets the same 250 harmful requests (JailbreakBench + HarmBench) in four forms: rewrites with the trigger\nwords removed vs. kept, 10 languages (MultiJail), role-play framings, and a 3-turn escalation. Refusal is also tested\nagainst keyword presence vs. a semantic harm rating, using XSTest prompts where the two diverge.\nAll outputs come from real models (vLLM, greedy decoding).\n\n**Full write-up: [REPORT.md](REPORT.md)**. Plan: [planning.md](planning.md).\n\n## Key findings\nSee REPORT.md §1 for the numbers. In short:\n- **Keyword removal works, but not as strongly as hypothesised.** Rewriting a harmful request without its trigger words\n  lowers refusal by about 10–28 pp (mean about 17 pp; 20–40 pp hypothesised). A plain re-wording that keeps the trigger\n  words costs only about 3 pp. Part of the drop comes from the rewrite sounding milder: on items where rated harm is\n  preserved, the drop is about 13 pp. The effect does **not** shrink with model size.\n- **Multilingual \"bypass\" is mostly incoherence, not jailbreak.** In sub-3B models, 44–86% of prompts refused in English\n  are not refused in another language. But only about 7–15% of them get a reply that actually carries out the request;\n  the rest are off-topic or garbled, especially in Bengali, Swahili and Javanese.\n- **Refusal tracks meaning more than keywords (H3 refuted).** In every model, r(refusal, trigger-word count) is\n  0.10–0.25, while r(refusal, semantic harm rating) is 0.27–0.64. With trigger words held fixed (XSTest unsafe vs. safe),\n  refusal differs by 44–82 pp; with meaning held fixed (keywords kept vs. removed), by 6–21 pp.\n- **Larger models keep systematic role-play blind spots (H4 supported).** Fiction framing raises compliance from ≤10% to\n  59–78% for Qwen2.5-3B/7B, Phi-3 and Gemma-2-9B. A \"no-rules persona\" prompt raises Gemma-2-9B from 2% to 86%. 3-turn\n  escalation is a smaller effect (≤24% for safety-tuned models ≥1.5B). Llama-3.x is the exception: it is robust to all\n  of these.\n\n## Reproduce\n```bash\nuv venv --python 3.12 && source .venv/bin/activate && uv sync      # vllm 0.32, pandas, scipy, statsmodels, matplotlib\ncd src\npython prep_data.py            # datasets/raw/*.csv -> harmful_base / controls / multijail jsonl\npython paraphrase.py           # keyword-free + control rewrites (needs an OpenAI-compatible Qwen3-8B at :8011)\npython build_prompts.py        # datasets/prompts.jsonl (5,450 prompts)\npython rate_prompts.py         # paraphrase equivalence + semantic harm ratings (Qwen3-8B)\nCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python worker.py ../logs/queue.txt   # one model key / task per line (see logs/queue_g1c.txt)\n./run_judges.sh                # Qwen3-8B judge (refusal + content questions); or task=judge_local in the worker\npython analysis.py             # tables -> results/tables, figures -> figures/, results/summary.json\npython examples.py             # qualitative examples -> results/examples.md\n```\nRaw datasets come from GitHub/HF (URLs in `datasets/README.md`). Models come from the local HF cache\n(`HF_HOME=/data/transformers`).\n\n## Layout\n```\nsrc/          common.py (paths, trigger lexicon) · models.py (14 targets) · prep_data/paraphrase/build_prompts/rate_prompts\n              worker.py + tasks.py (in-process vLLM worker for a shared GPU; generation, HarmBench classifier, local judge)\n              judge.py, run_judges.sh, llm_client.py (validated log-prob judge) · validate_judge*.py, gold_labels.py\n              analysis.py, examples.py\ndatasets/     raw/ (downloads), prompts.jsonl, paraphrases.jsonl, ...\nresults/      gens/ (raw model outputs — contain harmful text), judged/, hbcls/, tables/, summary.json, examples.md\nfigures/      e1_refusal_by_rewrite.png, e2_multilingual_*.png, e3_keyword_vs_harm.png, e4_roleplay_escalation.png\n```\n**Content warning:** `results/gens/` holds unfiltered model outputs to harmful requests, kept for research and auditing.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":451,"cache_read_input_tokens":263281,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":451},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0d6a03c-5885-4074-923e-9da3fb651629","uuid":"10f00fde-87e5-4f5f-928d-b1cd04984105","timestamp":"2026-10-27T19:48:48.718Z","request_id":"req_011CgTKYCNGkjy99YxSX2KCG","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_015MY9446EhADdJVZ1rjZUmh":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/gradient_masking_safety_20261027_120000_ab12cd34/README.md","content":"# Do safety-tuned LLMs refuse on keywords or on meaning?\n\nWe test whether safety tuning in open chat models (0.5B–32B; Qwen2.5, Qwen3, Llama-3.x, Llama-2, Phi-3, Mistral,\nGemma-2) works like \"gradient masking\": a defence keyed to surface patterns that simple distribution shifts get around.\nEvery model gets the same 250 harmful requests (JailbreakBench + HarmBench) in four forms: rewrites with the trigger\nwords removed vs. kept, 10 languages (MultiJail), role-play framings, and a 3-turn escalation. Refusal is also tested\nagainst keyword presence vs. a semantic harm rating, using XSTest prompts where the two diverge.\nAll outputs come from real models (vLLM, greedy decoding).\n\n**Full write-up: [REPORT.md](REPORT.md)**. Plan: [planning.md](planning.md).\n\n## Key findings\nSee REPORT.md §1 for the numbers. In short:\n- **Keyword removal works, but not as strongly as hypothesised.** Rewriting a harmful request without its trigger words\n  lowers refusal by about 10–28 pp (mean about 17 pp; 20–40 pp hypothesised). A plain re-wording that keeps the trigger\n  words costs only about 3 pp. Part of the drop comes from the rewrite sounding milder: on items where rated harm is\n  preserved, the drop is about 13 pp. The effect does **not** shrink with model size.\n- **Multilingual \"bypass\" is mostly incoherence, not jailbreak.** In sub-3B models, 44–86% of prompts refused in English\n  are not refused in another language. But only about 7–15% of them get a reply that actually carries out the request;\n  the rest are off-topic or garbled, especially in Bengali, Swahili and Javanese.\n- **Refusal tracks meaning more than keywords (H3 refuted).** In every model, r(refusal, trigger-word count) is\n  0.10–0.25, while r(refusal, semantic harm rating) is 0.27–0.64. With trigger words held fixed (XSTest unsafe vs. safe),\n  refusal differs by 44–82 pp; with meaning held fixed (keywords kept vs. removed), by 6–21 pp.\n- **Larger models keep systematic role-play blind spots (H4 supported).** Fiction framing raises compliance from ≤10% to\n  59–78% for Qwen2.5-3B/7B, Phi-3 and Gemma-2-9B. A \"no-rules persona\" prompt raises Gemma-2-9B from 2% to 86%. 3-turn\n  escalation is a smaller effect (≤24% for safety-tuned models ≥1.5B). Llama-3.x is the exception: it is robust to all\n  of these.\n\n## Reproduce\n```bash\nuv venv --python 3.12 && source .venv/bin/activate && uv sync      # vllm 0.32, pandas, scipy, statsmodels, matplotlib\ncd src\npython prep_data.py            # datasets/raw/*.csv -> harmful_base / controls / multijail jsonl\npython paraphrase.py           # keyword-free + control rewrites (needs an OpenAI-compatible Qwen3-8B at :8011)\npython build_prompts.py        # datasets/prompts.jsonl (5,450 prompts)\npython rate_prompts.py         # paraphrase equivalence + semantic harm ratings (Qwen3-8B)\nCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python worker.py ../logs/queue.txt   # one model key / task per line (see logs/queue_g1c.txt)\n./run_judges.sh                # Qwen3-8B judge (refusal + content questions); or task=judge_local in the worker\npython analysis.py             # tables -> results/tables, figures -> figures/, results/summary.json\npython examples.py             # qualitative examples -> results/examples.md\n```\nRaw datasets come from GitHub/HF (URLs in `datasets/README.md`). Models come from the local HF cache\n(`HF_HOME=/data/transformers`).\n\n## Layout\n```\nsrc/          common.py (paths, trigger lexicon) · models.py (14 targets) · prep_data/paraphrase/build_prompts/rate_prompts\n              worker.py + tasks.py (in-process vLLM worker for a shared GPU; generation, HarmBench classifier, local judge)\n              judge.py, run_judges.sh, llm_client.py (validated log-prob judge) · validate_judge*.py, gold_labels.py\n              analysis.py, examples.py\ndatasets/     raw/ (downloads), prompts.jsonl, paraphrases.jsonl, ...\nresults/      gens/ (raw model outputs — contain harmful text), judged/, hbcls/, tables/, summary.json, examples.md\nfigures/      e1_refusal_by_rewrite.png, e2_multilingual_*.png, e3_keyword_vs_harm.png, e4_roleplay_escalation.png\n```\n**Content warning:** `results/gens/` holds unfiltered model outputs to harmful requests, kept for research and auditing.\n"}}}
