{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTMRQt8FcjvdwLDroQto","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YTrffz7j4T3PCNji6nyRtJ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - << 'EOF'\np='REPORT_body.md'; s=open(p).read()\nold=s[s.index('4. **Role-play and escalation (H4'):s.index('**Practical implication.**')]\nnew='''4. **Role-play and escalation (H4, supported for role-play; scale helps only within a family).** Models with robust\n   direct refusals have large, model-specific blind spots under one-paragraph framings:\n   - *Fiction* raises compliance from ≤ 11% to 51–78% in Qwen2.5-1.5B/3B/7B and Phi-3-mini, to 59% in Gemma-2-9B and\n     to 43% in Llama-3.1-8B.\n   - A *\"no-rules persona\"* raises Llama-3.1-8B from 14% to 93% and Gemma-2-9B from 1.6% to 86%.\n   - *Three-turn escalation* is a much weaker attack (≤ 24% compliance for every model except the barely-aligned\n     Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-0.5B and Mistral-7B).\n   - Within Qwen2.5, scale closes the fiction gap ({{QWEN_FICTION_TREND}}), but the 8–9B models of other families are\n     among the most exploitable. Robustness therefore depends on the alignment recipe more than on parameter count.\n\n'''\ns=s.replace(old,new)\nold=s[s.index('**H4: framing blind spots are large'):s.index('**Overall interpretation.**')]\nnew='''**H4: framing blind spots are large and family-specific; scale helps only within a family.**\n- Fiction framing is the strongest single attack on Qwen2.5 1.5B–7B and Phi-3: compliance goes from ~5–10% direct to\n  51–78%, with near-identical HarmBench-harmful rates, so these are real harmful completions, not vague stories.\n- The persona prompt breaks Llama-3.1-8B (93%) and Gemma-2-9B (86%). Gemma-2-9B is otherwise the most conservative\n  model (95.5% refusal on direct requests).\n- Llama-3.2-1B/3B stay ≤ 14% under every framing. Qwen2.5-14B stays ≤ 11%{{QWEN32_NOTE}}.\n- 3-turn escalation is weaker than single-turn framing for every safety-tuned model.\n- So the hypothesis that larger models generalise better holds *within* the Qwen2.5 family (7B → 14B: fiction 71% →\n  11%), but not across families: the two most persona-vulnerable models are 8–9B. Every family we tested except\n  Llama-3.2 has at least one simple framing that defeats a model in the 1.5–9B range.\n\n'''\ns=s.replace(old,new)\ns=s.replace('''safety-tuned models ≥ 1.5B). Llama-3.x is the exception: it is robust to all\n  of these.''','''safety-tuned models ≥ 1.5B).''')\ns=s.replace('''gaps are *framing* gaps (fiction, personas) and a residual lexical sensitivity of 10–15 pp, and neither closes with\nscale within a model family.''','''gaps are *framing* gaps (fiction, personas) and a residual lexical sensitivity of 10–15 pp. The lexical sensitivity\ndoes not shrink with scale, and the framing gaps close with scale only inside some families (Qwen2.5).''')\ns=s.replace('''incoherence. The real, exploitable blind spots are **framing-specific** (fiction, personas): large, family-dependent,\nand not fixed by scale.''','''incoherence. The real, exploitable blind spots are **framing-specific** (fiction, personas): large, family-dependent,\nand closed by scale only inside some families.''')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"Llama-3.x\" REPORT_body.md README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1923,"cache_read_input_tokens":293871,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1923},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0d6a03c-5885-4074-923e-9da3fb651629","uuid":"68469de3-6eed-4556-9a00-1d88eb79d939","timestamp":"2026-10-27T20:13:26.647Z","request_id":"req_011CgTMRQdFd4XPUBpQpAsEi","wire_tool_inputs":{"t