{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTNQvRyfDs3sKUjSAY7o","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xyjek4LywdVg5RY7y7ZD3t","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/gradient_masking_safety_20261027_120000_ab12cd34 && python3 - << 'EOF'\np='REPORT_body.md'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"**Role-play and escalation (H4, supported for role-play; scale helps only within a family).**\",\"**Role-play and escalation (H4, supported for role-play; not a scale effect).**\")\ns=s.replace(\"   - Within Qwen2.5, fiction vulnerability peaks at 3–7B and largely disappears at 14B ({{QWEN_FICTION_TREND}}).\\n     But the 8–9B models of other families are among the most exploitable.\",\n            \"   - Within Qwen2.5, fiction vulnerability is non-monotonic in size ({{QWEN_FICTION_TREND}}), and the 8–9B models\\n     of other families are among the most exploitable.\")\nold=s[s.index('**H4: framing blind spots are large and family-specific'):s.index('**Overall interpretation.**')]\nnew='''**H4: framing blind spots are large, family-specific, and not removed by scale.**\n- Fiction framing is the strongest single attack on Qwen2.5 1.5B–7B and Phi-3: compliance goes from ~5–10% direct to\n  51–78%, with near-identical HarmBench-harmful rates, so these are real harmful completions, not vague stories.\n- The persona prompt breaks Llama-3.1-8B (93%) and Gemma-2-9B (86%). Gemma-2-9B is otherwise the most conservative\n  model (95.5% refusal on direct requests).\n- Llama-3.2-1B/3B stay ≤ 14% under every framing, and Qwen2.5-14B ≤ 11%. Yet Qwen2.5-32B is again vulnerable to\n  fiction (40%).\n- 3-turn escalation is weaker than single-turn framing for every safety-tuned model.\n- The hypothesis that larger models generalise better is therefore not supported. Within Qwen2.5, fiction compliance\n  is non-monotonic in size ({{QWEN_FICTION_TREND}}). Across families, the two most persona-vulnerable models are\n  8–9B. Robustness to framing looks like a property of the specific alignment recipe and checkpoint; it is not an\n  emergent property of scale. Every family we tested except Llama-3.2 has a simple framing that defeats at least one of\n  its models.\n\n'''\ns=s.replace(old,new)\ns=s.replace('''The lexical sensitivity\ndoes not shrink with scale, and the framing gaps close with scale only inside some families (Qwen2.5).''','''Neither shrinks\nreliably with scale.''')\ns=s.replace('''and closed by scale only inside some families.''','''and not reliably closed by scale (Qwen2.5-32B is more fiction-vulnerable than Qwen2.5-14B).''')\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='README.md'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"(both ≤14% when asked directly). Within Qwen2.5, the gap closes at 14B;\\n  Llama-3.2-1B/3B are robust to every framing.\",\"(both ≤14% when asked directly). Within Qwen2.5, fiction compliance is non-monotonic\\n  (7B 71% → 14B 11% → 32B 40%). Llama-3.2-1B/3B are robust to every framing.\")\ns=s.replace(\"(H4 supported, but not as a clean scale effect)\",\"(H4 supported for role-play; not a scale effect)\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"non-monotonic\" README.md REPORT_body.md | cut -c1-120; grep -E \"^[0-9]{2}:\" logs/worker_g1c.log | tail -1"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1573,"cache_read_input_tokens":303866,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1573},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0d6a03c-5885-4074-923e-9da3fb651629","uuid":"b9659c2a-6a65-4e28-8b0d-6751d90a7f9c","timestamp":"2026-10-27T20:26:22.042Z","request_id":"req_011CgTNQutE9sbncHjXxLWQD","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Xyjek4LywdVg5RY7y7ZD3t":{"command