{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011QHbwLGfRh4WfafCESnkW9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Gradient Masking in Safety-Tuned Models: Do Alignment Methods Create Exploitable Blind Spots?\n2\t\n3\t*All numbers below come from real generations of 13 open-weight chat models (greedy decoding, vLLM 0.32) on\n4\t5,450 prompts each (4,190 for models ≥ 7B, see §3.6), labelled by a validated Qwen3-8B judge and the official HarmBench\n5\tclassifier. Tables are generated by `src/make_tables.py` from `results/tables/*.csv`.*\n6\t\n7\t## 1. Executive summary\n8\t\n9\t**Question.** Does safety tuning teach models a *surface* refusal reflex (trigger words, English, direct phrasing) and\n10\tnot a *semantic* notion of harm? That would be the LLM analogue of gradient masking: a defence that looks robust on the\n11\tinputs it was tuned on but fails under simple distribution shifts.\n12\t\n13\t**Answer: partly, and less than hypothesised.** We tested 13 models from 0.5B to 32.8B on the same 250\n14\tharmful requests.\n15\t\n16\t1. **Keyword removal (H1, partly supported).** Rewriting a request so it asks for the same thing without trigger words\n17\t   lowers refusal by **16.3 pp on average** (range 4.5–27.5 pp; significant in 12/13 models). A matched\n18\t   re-wording that keeps the trigger words lowers it by only 2.5 pp, so most of the drop is lexical. But only\n19\t   3 of 13 models fall in the hypothesised 20–40 pp band. Part of the effect is that the keyword-free\n20\t   rewrite *sounds* milder: on items where an LLM rater judges it just as harmful, the drop is 12.0 pp. And the\n21\t   HarmBench-harmful completion rate rises by only 3.0 pp, because many non-refusals are watered-down answers.\n22\t2. **Multilingual bypass (H2, mostly refuted under a strict definition).** For sub-3B models, 44–78% of the\n23\t   MultiJail prompts refused in English are *not refused* in other languages (in the hypothesised 40–60% range). But\n24\t   only 6.6–14.8% get a reply that actually carries out the request. Most \"bypasses\" are off-topic, misunderstood\n25\t   or garbled replies, especially in low-resource languages (Bengali, Swahili, Javanese).\n26\t3. **Keyword vs. semantic harm (H3, refuted).** No model's refusal correlates more with trigger-word count than with\n27\t   semantic harm. r(refusal, keywords) is 0.10–0.25; r(refusal, harm rating) is 0.27–0.63. Controlled contrasts give\n28\t   the same answer: with trigger words held fixed, refusal moves 44–82 pp between unsafe and safe XSTest prompts;\n29\t   with meaning held fixed, it moves 6–21 pp between keyword-kept and keyword-free rewrites. Small models are not\n30\t   more keyword-driven than large ones.\n31\t4. **Role-play and escalation (H4, supported for role-play; not a scale effect).** Models with robust\n32\t   direct refusals have large, model-specific blind spots under one-paragraph framings:\n33\t   - *Fiction* raises compliance from ≤ 11% to 51–78% in Qwen2.5-1.5B/3B/7B and Phi-3-mini, to 59% in Gemma-2-9B and\n34\t     to 43% in Llama-3.1-8B.\n35\t   - A *\"no-rules persona\"* raises Llama-3.1-8B from 14% to 93% and Gemma-2-9B from 1.6% to 86%.\n36\t   - *Three-turn escalation* is a much weaker attack (≤ 24% compliance for every model except the barely-aligned\n37\t     Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-0.5B and Mistral-7B).\n38\t   - Within Qwen2.5, fiction vulnerability is non-monotonic in size (fiction compliance 0.5B 30% → 1.5B 51% → 3.1B 76% → 7.6B 71% → 14.8B 11% → 32.8B 40%), and the 8–9B models\n39\t     of other families are among the most exploitable. Robustness therefore depends on the alignment recipe more than on parameter count.\n40\t\n41\t**Practical implication.** Safety tuning in these models is mostly semantic, not keyword-matching. The exploitable\n42\tgaps are *framing* gaps (fiction, personas) and a residual lexical sensitivity of 10–15 pp. Neither shrinks\n43\treliably with scale. Red-teaming should prioritise framing attacks. Multilingual \"jailbreak rates\" for small\n44\tmodels should be measured as harmful compliance, not non-refusal, or they are inflated several-fold.\n45\t\n46\t## 2. Research question & motivation\n47\t\n48\tHypotheses (from the task statement):\n49\t- **H1** Paraphrasing harmful requests to avoid trigger words lowers refusal by 20–40%.\n50\t- **H2** Multi-language probes bypass safety in sub-3B models 40–60% of the time.\n51\t- **H3** In small models, refusal correlates more with keyword presence (r > 0.6) than with semantic harm (r < 0.3).\n52\t- **H4** Larger models generalise better semantically but keep systematic blind spots in role-play and escalation.\n53\t\n54\tWhy it matters: if refusal is lexical, deployed small models (on-device, open-weight) can be bypassed by anyone able to\n55\tre-phrase, and their over-refusals on benign prompts with trigger words cost usability. The gap in prior work (§2.1) is\n56\ta *controlled* separation of keyword effects from paraphrase and meaning effects, measured on the same items across\n57\tmodel sizes.\n58\t\n59\t### 2.1 Related work (background; the resource-finder stage was skipped, so no pre-collected papers were available)\n60\t- *Multilingual jailbreaks*: Deng et al. 2024 (MultiJail) and Yong et al. 2023 report much higher unsafe rates in\n61\t  low-resource languages, and Deng et al. note that low-capability models often produce *invalid* (irrelevant) output.\n62\t  Our finding refines this for small models.\n63\t- *Reformulation attacks*: Andriushchenko & Flammarion 2024 (past-tense rewrites), PAIR (Chao et al. 2023) and\n64\t  persuasive paraphrasing (Zeng et al. 2024) show that re-wording jailbreaks models. We add a keyword-kept control so the\n65\t  lexical component can be isolated.\n66\t- *Exaggerated safety*: XSTest (Röttger et al. 2024) shows over-refusal on prompts with trigger words; we use it as the\n67\t  \"keywords without harm\" arm.\n68\t- *Role-play / multi-turn*: DAN-style personas (Shen et al. 2024), Crescendo (Russinovich et al. 2024).\n69\t- *Evaluation*: HarmBench (Mazeika et al. 2024) classifier and behaviours; JailbreakBench (Chao et al. 2024).\n70\t\n71\t## 3. Methodology\n72\t\n73\t### 3.1 Models (all local, vLLM 0.32, bf16 or AWQ-INT4, greedy, max 256 new tokens, default chat template, no system prompt)\n74\t| key | HF checkpoint | params (B) | weights |\n75\t|---|---|---|---|\n76\t| qwen2.5-0.5b | `Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct` | 0.5 | bf16 |\n77\t| qwen3-0.6b | `Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B` | 0.6 | bf16 |\n78\t| llama3.2-1b | `unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct` | 1.2 | bf16 |\n79\t| qwen2.5-1.5b | `Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct` | 1.5 | bf16 |\n80\t| qwen2.5-3b | `Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct` | 3.1 | bf16 |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0d6a03c-5885-4074-923e-9da3fb651629","uuid":"1d7e65e1-421b-4f5d-a2d6-0c7b35f2c5ab","timestamp":"2026-10-27T20:26:53.173Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/gradient_masking_safety_20261027_120000_ab12cd34/REPORT.md","content":"# Gradient Masking in Safety-Tuned Models: Do Alignment Methods Create Exploitable Blind Spots?\n\n*All numbers below come from real generations of 13 open-weight chat models (greedy decoding, vLLM 0.32) on\n5,450 prompts each (4,190 for models ≥ 7B, see §3.6), labelled by a validated Qwen3-8B judge and the official HarmBench\nclassifier. Tables are generated by `src/make_tables.py` from `results/tables/*.csv`.*\n\n## 1. Executive summary\n\n**Question.** Does safety tuning teach models a *surface* refusal reflex (trigger words, English, direct phrasing) and\nnot a *semantic* notion of harm? That would be the LLM analogue of gradient masking: a defence that looks robust on the\ninputs it was tuned on but fails under simple distribution shifts.\n\n**Answer: partly, and less than hypothesised.** We tested 13 models from 0.5B to 32.8B on the same 250\nharmful requests.\n\n1. **Keyword removal (H1, partly supported).** Rewriting a request so it asks for the same thing without trigger words\n   lowers refusal by **16.3 pp on average** (range 4.5–27.5 pp; significant in 12/13 models). A matched\n   re-wording that keeps the trigger words lowers it by only 2.5 pp, so most of the drop is lexical. But only\n   3 of 13 models fall in the hypothesised 20–40 pp band. Part of the effect is that the keyword-free\n   rewrite *sounds* milder: on items where an LLM rater judges it just as harmful, the drop is 12.0 pp. And the\n   HarmBench-harmful completion rate rises by only 3.0 pp, because many non-refusals are watered-down answers.\n2. **Multilingual bypass (H2, mostly refuted under a strict definition).** For sub-3B models, 44–78% of the\n   MultiJail prompts refused in English are *not refused* in other languages (in the hypothesised 40–60% range). But\n   only 6.6–14.8% get a reply that actually carries out the request. Most \"bypasses\" are off-topic, misunderstood\n   or garbled replies, especially in low-resource languages (Bengali, Swahili, Javanese).\n3. **Keyword vs. semantic harm (H3, refuted).** No model's refusal correlates more with trigger-word count than with\n   semantic harm. r(refusal, keywords) is 0.10–0.25; r(refusal, harm rating) is 0.27–0.63. Controlled contrasts give\n   the same answer: with trigger words held fixed, refusal moves 44–82 pp between unsafe and safe XSTest prompts;\n   with meaning held fixed, it moves 6–21 pp between keyword-kept and keyword-free rewrites. Small models are not\n   more keyword-driven than large ones.\n4. **Role-play and escalation (H4, supported for role-play; not a scale effect).** Models with robust\n   direct refusals have large, model-specific blind spots under one-paragraph framings:\n   - *Fiction* raises compliance from ≤ 11% to 51–78% in Qwen2.5-1.5B/3B/7B and Phi-3-mini, to 59% in Gemma-2-9B and\n     to 43% in Llama-3.1-8B.\n   - A *\"no-rules persona\"* raises Llama-3.1-8B from 14% to 93% and Gemma-2-9B from 1.6% to 86%.\n   - *Three-turn escalation* is a much weaker attack (≤ 24% compliance for every model except the barely-aligned\n     Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-0.5B and Mistral-7B).\n   - Within Qwen2.5, fiction vulnerability is non-monotonic in size (fiction compliance 0.5B 30% → 1.5B 51% → 3.1B 76% → 7.6B 71% → 14.8B 11% → 32.8B 40%), and the 8–9B models\n     of other families are among the most exploitable. Robustness therefore depends on the alignment recipe more than on parameter count.\n\n**Practical implication.** Safety tuning in these models is mostly semantic, not keyword-matching. The exploitable\ngaps are *framing* gaps (fiction, personas) and a residual lexical sensitivity of 10–15 pp. Neither shrinks\nreliably with scale. Red-teaming should prioritise framing attacks. Multilingual \"jailbreak rates\" for small\nmodels should be measured as harmful compliance, not non-refusal, or they are inflated several-fold.\n\n## 2. Research question & motivation\n\nHypotheses (from the task statement):\n- **H1** Paraphrasing harmful requests to avoid trigger words lowers refusal by 20–40%.\n- **H2** Multi-language probes bypass safety in sub-3B models 40–60% of the time.\n- **H3** In small models, refusal correlates more with keyword presence (r > 0.6) than with semantic harm (r < 0.3).\n- **H4** Larger models generalise better semantically but keep systematic blind spots in role-play and escalation.\n\nWhy it matters: if refusal is lexical, deployed small models (on-device, open-weight) can be bypassed by anyone able to\nre-phrase, and their over-refusals on benign prompts with trigger words cost usability. The gap in prior work (§2.1) is\na *controlled* separation of keyword effects from paraphrase and meaning effects, measured on the same items across\nmodel sizes.\n\n### 2.1 Related work (background; the resource-finder stage was skipped, so no pre-collected papers were available)\n- *Multilingual jailbreaks*: Deng et al. 2024 (MultiJail) and Yong et al. 2023 report much higher unsafe rates in\n  low-resource languages, and Deng et al. note that low-capability models often produce *invalid* (irrelevant) output.\n  Our finding refines this for small models.\n- *Reformulation attacks*: Andriushchenko & Flammarion 2024 (past-tense rewrites), PAIR (Chao et al. 2023) and\n  persuasive paraphrasing (Zeng et al. 2024) show that re-wording jailbreaks models. We add a keyword-kept control so the\n  lexical component can be isolated.\n- *Exaggerated safety*: XSTest (Röttger et al. 2024) shows over-refusal on prompts with trigger words; we use it as the\n  \"keywords without harm\" arm.\n- *Role-play / multi-turn*: DAN-style personas (Shen et al. 2024), Crescendo (Russinovich et al. 2024).\n- *Evaluation*: HarmBench (Mazeika et al. 2024) classifier and behaviours; JailbreakBench (Chao et al. 2024).\n\n## 3. Methodology\n\n### 3.1 Models (all local, vLLM 0.32, bf16 or AWQ-INT4, greedy, max 256 new tokens, default chat template, no system prompt)\n| key | HF checkpoint | params (B) | weights |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| qwen2.5-0.5b | `Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct` | 0.5 | bf16 |\n| qwen3-0.6b | `Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B` | 0.6 | bf16 |\n| llama3.2-1b | `unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct` | 1.2 | bf16 |\n| qwen2.5-1.5b | `Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct` | 1.5 | bf16 |\n| qwen2.5-3b | `Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct` | 3.1 | bf16 |","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":437}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011QHbwLGfRh4WfafCESnkW9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
